  "rules": {
    "Local.0001": {
      "id": "Local.0001",
      "description": "Windows local system features usage detected.\n\nManaged Instance on App Service allows use of registry adapters (Key Vault-backed) for config; replace local process launching with Functions/Jobs; route file I/O to storage mounts. Use Bastion RDP only for diagnostics and persist changes via scripts.\n\nWindows features like registry access, starting processes via shell, access to COM objects, using standard OS dialogs etc, will be restricted on Azure App Service. This indicates that your application might be broken in App Service sandbox environment or not scale well.\n\nIf you choose to use containerized Azure hosting options with Windows container, such as Azure AppService sandbox for Windows container, Azure Container Apps (ACA) or Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS), some scenarios might work, however scaling still might be an issue.\n\nRecommendations:\n    1. Avoid using Windows registry or default Windows UI elements and dialogs.\n    2. If your application needs to read app-specific configuration, you can use the App Settings feature of Azure App Service, which allows you to store key-value pairs of configuration data for your application. You can access these settings from your code using environment variables. Another option is to use Azure App Configuration and Azure Key Vault to share application settings in a cloud-native way.\n    3. If your application is writing data (like machine-wide settings cache), use an external data store like Azure SQL DB or Azure Cache for Redis.\n    4. Instead of starting local worker processes use Azure Functions.",
      "title": "Windows local system features usage detected",
      "severity": "mandatory",
      "effort": 3,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "[Managed Instance] Registry adapters (Key Vault-backed)",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2347058"
        },
        {
          "title": "[Managed Instance] Storage mounts",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2346952"
        },
        {
          "title": "[Managed Instance] Bastion RDP",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2346953"
        },
        {
          "title": "General App Service sandbox restrictions",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250496"
        },
        {
          "title": "App Service sandbox for Windows Container Web Apps",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2251963"
        },
        {
          "title": "Design to scale out",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2251007"
        },
        {
          "title": "Azure Functions",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2251810"
        },
        {
          "title": "App Settings",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2242590"
        },
        {
          "title": "Azure Key Vault",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2251612"
        },
        {
          "title": "Azure App Configuration",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2251640"
        }
      ],
      "labels": []
    },
    "Local.0014": {
      "id": "Local.0014",
      "description": "Writing or modifying diagnostics in Windows Event Log detected.\n\nPrefer Application Insights/Azure Monitor. Managed Instance on Azure App Service allows diagnostics via Bastion RDP (Event Viewer), but persist telemetry to cloud sinks; RDP changes are not persistent.\n\nWhile if targeting other Azure compute services, writing diagnostics to the event log is an antipattern for Azure-hosted services. Although it\u0027s possible to view event log messages in Azure App Service (via diagnostic tools) and Azure Kubernetes Service (via LogMonitor), there are much better logging options, like Azure Monitor and Application Insights, that will be more productive.",
      "title": "Writing or modifying diagnostics in Windows Event Log detected",
      "severity": "optional",
      "effort": 3,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "Azure App Service monitoring",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250851"
        },
        {
          "title": "Application Insights overview",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250852"
        },
        {
          "title": "Capture logs with App Service Diagnostics training",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250853"
        },
        {
          "title": "LogMonitor",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2251308"
        }
      ],
      "labels": []
    },
    "Identity.0002": {
      "id": "Identity.0002",
      "description": "Your app is using windows authentication which is not supported on Azure App Service, Azure Container Apps and Azure Kubernetes Service.\n\nAzure App Service provides built-in authentication and authorization capabilities, so you can sign in users and access data by writing minimal or no code in your web app.\n\nFollow the link below for more information.\u0022",
      "title": "Windows authentication detected",
      "severity": "mandatory",
      "effort": 3,
      "links": [
        {
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2242883"
        }
      ],
      "labels": []
    },
    "Scale.0002": {
      "id": "Scale.0002",
      "description": "Session state stored in-proc or in local process is detected.\n\nIf you are targeting Managed Instance on Azure App Service, move session to Azure Cache for Redis or Cosmos DB. In-proc session impedes scale-out; Managed Instance does not change session semantics.\n\nWhile if targeting other Azure compute services:\n\nIn-proc or local process stored session state not immediately prevents an application from working in Azure, however, is not recommended because it would make scaling the Azure App Service or Azure Kubernetes Service more difficult.\n\nAzure provides multiple ways to store session state in a scalable way using Azure Cache for Redis, Azure Cosmos DB or other services.",
      "title": "Session state stored in-proc or in local process is detected",
      "severity": "potential",
      "effort": 3,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "ASP.NET Session State Provider for Azure Cache for Redis",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250855"
        },
        {
          "title": "Use Azure Cosmos DB as an ASP.NET session state and caching provider",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250757"
        },
        {
          "title": "Azure Cosmos DB: autoscale, session state, monitoring, and more",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250758"
        },
        {
          "title": "Design to scale out",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2251007"
        }
      ],
      "labels": []
    },
    "Security.0002": {
      "id": "Security.0002",
      "description": "Connection strings without configuration builders detected.  \n\n Keeping connection strings directly in the app is not the best practice and might introduce security risks, increased maintenance, scalability challenges or compliance standards (such as PCI DSS, GDPR, etc.). \n\n Instead, it is recommended to use configuration builders to get secrets from secure sources in Azure environment.",
      "title": "Connection strings without configuration builders detected",
      "severity": "optional",
      "effort": 3,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "Configuration builders",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2250915"
        },
        {
          "title": "Storing application secrets",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2250916"
        },
        {
          "title": "Centralized app configuration and security",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2250733"
        }
      ],
      "labels": []
    },
    "Local.0003": {
      "id": "Local.0003",
      "description": "Your application has local or network IO operations.\n\nIf you are targeting Managed Instance on Azure App Service, move persistent I/O to storage mounts (Azure Files/UNC). Keep local writes to temp only; connect to external dependencies via private endpoints/VNet to reduce latency.\n\nIO operations outside of the application folder might not be available on Azure App Service. Review and ensure IO APIs are attempting to access paths that will be available when your app is running on App Service sandbox. If you choose the containerized hosting option, your scenario still might be broken or have scalability issues.\n\nPossible solutions:\n    1. Files that the application needs can be deployed with the application or put in Azure Storage. You can use Azure Storage File Shares as a drop-in replacement for on-premises file share.\n    2. Other services that the application depends on might need to be migrated to Azure, this way you will get the benefit of lower latency in the cloud.\n    3. If your application needs to depend on services that run on-premise, you can connect your Azure and local networks with ExpressRoute, Hybrid Connections, or VPN solutions.",
      "title": "Local or network IO operations detected",
      "severity": "potential",
      "effort": 3,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "[Managed Instance] Storage mounts",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2346952"
        },
        {
          "title": "Azure file share",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2242591"
        },
        {
          "title": "ExpressRoute",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2250577"
        },
        {
          "title": "Hybrid Connections",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2242588"
        },
        {
          "title": "Connect an on-prem network",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2242591"
        },
        {
          "title": "Design to scale out",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2251007"
        }
      ],
      "labels": []
    },
    "Local.0007": {
      "id": "Local.0007",
      "description": "Local OS environment access detected.\n\nIf you are targeting Managed Instance on Azure App Service, Read config via App Settings or App Configuration; avoid machine\u2011specific environment APIs. Persist environment setup via install scripts when needed.\n\nWhen running in Azure App Service environment, your application can read environment variables that you define in App Service settings. Using environment API other than reading environment variables might indicate that your application depends on some local machine\u0027s features and might be broken on App Service sandbox or not scale well. Review your scenarios and either refactor them to be more cloud friendly or choose the option in App Service to host your application in the sandbox for Windows Container Web Apps.\n\nIf you choose to use other Azure hosting options such as Azure Container Apps (ACA) or Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS), environment APIs might work, however scaling still might be an issue.",
      "title": "Local OS environment access detected",
      "severity": "potential",
      "effort": 1,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "General App Service sandbox restrictions",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250496"
        },
        {
          "title": "App Service sandbox for Windows Container Web Apps",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2251963"
        },
        {
          "title": "Design to scale out",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2251007"
        }
      ],
      "labels": []
    }
